# This is based on text found at https://eev.ee/blog/2012/04/09/php-a-fractal-of-bad-design/
# which enumerates the author's issues with PHP interal inconsistencies.
#
# Issues in the text are, where appropriate, replicated in VGR script to show
# the behavior and explain VGR's behavior.
#
# The goal is neither to be like PHP nor to have behavior matching the author's
# desire, but to discover and address issues that a script author may find astonishing.

# Operators
# == is useless.
# It’s not transitive. "foo" == TRUE, and "foo" == 0… but, of course, TRUE != 0.
Assert "foo" == TRUE                   # VGR: follows truthiness rules of a string
Assert ("foo" == 0) == FALSE           # VGR: string comparison, zero cast to string
Assert TRUE != 0                       # VGR: boolean comparison, zero cast to boolean

# == converts to numbers when possible (123 == "123foo"… although "123" != "123foo"),
# which means it converts to floats when possible.
Assert (123 == "123foo") == FALSE      # VGR: "123foo" is NOT convertable to a number, so it is a string comparison

# So large hex strings (like, say, password hashes) may occasionally
# compare true when they’re not. Even JavaScript doesn’t do this.
# NB- no PHP example provided by author

# For the same reason, "6" == " 6", "4.2" == "4.20", and "133" == "0133".
Assert ("6" == " 6") == FALSE          # VGR: string comparison becuase of first operator
Assert ("4.2" == "4.20") == FALSE      # VGR: string comparison becuase of first operator
Assert ("133" == "0133") == FALSE      # VGR: string comparison becuase of first operator
Assert 6 == " 6"                       # VGR: when asked to compare a string/number combination,
Assert " 6" == 6                       #      we first try to convert the string to a number and
Assert 4.2 == "4.20"                   #      then compare the result. If it cannot be converted,
Assert "4.20" == 4.2                   #      the number is converted to a string and then
Assert 133 == "0133"                   #      the comparison is performed. This preserves
Assert "0133" == 133                   #      the transience of inequalities.

# But note that 133 != 0133, because 0133 is octal.
Assert 133 == 0133                     # VGR: both are decimal values
Assert 133 != 0o133                    # VGR: second operand is octal representation

# But "0x10" == "16"
Assert ("0x10" == "16") == FALSE       # VGR: string comparison
Assert (16 == "0x10") == FALSE         # VGR: only implicit decimal conversion
Assert "0x10".ParseHex() == "16"       # VGR: explicit parsing
# and "1e3" == "1000"!
Assert ("1e3" == "1000") == FALSE      # VGR: string comparison
Assert 1000 == "1e3"                   # VGR: implicit cast to a number
Assert "1e3".ToFloat() == "1000"       # VGR: explicit cast

# === compares values and type… except with objects, where === is only true if both operands
# are actually the same object! For objects, == compares both value (of every attribute) and type,
# which is what === does for every other type. What.
Assert [0] == ["0"]                    # VGR: length and elements are equivalent
Assert ([0] === ["0"]) == FALSE        # VGR: should use === on the elements within

# Comparison isn’t much better.
# It’s not even consistent: NULL < -1, and NULL == 0.
Assert Null < -1                       # VGR: None is smaller than (most) anything
Assert (Null == 0) == FALSE            # VGR: Null/None only equal to itself
Assert (0 == Null) == FALSE
Assert (Null == FALSE) == FALSE
Assert (False == Null) == FALSE

# Sorting is thus nondeterministic; it depends on the order in which the sort algorithm happens
# to compare elements.
Assert [0, "0", 1].Sort() == [0, "0", 1] # VGR: heterogeneous stable, but can be unpredictable when there
Assert ["0", 0, 1].Sort() == ["0", 0, 1] #      are are equivalent but not identical strings and numbers

# The comparison operators try to sort arrays, two different ways:
# first by length, then by elements. If they have the same number of elements but different sets of keys,
# though, they are uncomparable.
# VGR: can only compare dictionaries for equality, not </> et al

# Objects compare as greater than anything else… except other objects,
# which they are neither less than nor greater than.
# VGR: not applicable

# For a more type-safe ==, we have ===.
Assert 123 == "123"                    # VGR: second operand converted to an integer
Assert (123 === "123") == False        # VGR: requires both type and value match

# For a more type-safe <, we have… nothing. "123" < "0124", always, no matter what you do.
Assert ("123" < "0124") == FALSE       # VGR: both operands are strings, so string comparison performed
Assert 123 < "0124"                    # VGR: numeric comparison as first operand is numeric and second can be converted to an integer

# Casting doesn’t help, either.
Assert "123".ToInteger() < "0124".ToInteger() # VGR: explicit casts work as expected

# Despite the craziness above, and the explicit rejection of Perl’s pairs of string and numeric operators,
# PHP does not overload +. + is always addition, and . is always concatenation.
Assert (5 + 6) == 11                   # VGR: plus as addition
Assert (5 + "6") == 11                 # VGR: addition because first operand is numeric
Assert ("5" + 6) == "56"               # VGR: concatenation because first operand is a string
Assert ("5" + "6") == "56"             # VGR: plus as concatenation (strings, arrays, dictionaries)

# The [] indexing operator can also be spelled {}.
# VGR: not applicable, {} is a dictionary

# [] can be used on any variable, not just strings and arrays. It returns null and issues no warning.
Assert Null[0] == Null                 # VGR: anything can be treated as a list
Assert 123[0] == 123                   # VGR: same as above

# [] cannot slice; it only retrieves individual elements.
Assert "foo"[0:1] == "f"               # VGR: slicing as per Python semantics
Assert "fox"[:1] == "f"
Assert "fox"[::1] == "fox"
Assert "fox"[::-1] == "xof"

# foo()[0] is a syntax error. (Fixed in PHP 5.4.)
Assert Upper("foo")[0] == 'F'          # VGR: indexing treated like a function
Assert Upper("foo")[:1] == 'F'         # VGR: slicing treated like a function

# Unlike (literally!) every other language with a similar operator, ?: is left associative. So this:
Set x = 'T';
Set vehicle = ( ( x == 'B' ) ? 'bus' :
                ( x == 'A' ) ? 'airplane' :
                ( x == 'T' ) ? 'train' :
                ( x == 'C' ) ? 'car' :
                ( x == 'H' ) ? 'horse' :
                'feet' );
# prints horse.
Assert vehicle == "train"                          # VGR: ?: is left associative

# Variables
# There is no way to declare a variable.
Assert glob1 Is Not Defined
Declare glob1
Assert glob1 Is Defined

# Variables that don’t exist are created with a null value when first used.
Unset glob1; Assert glob1 Is Not Defined           # VGR: variable no longer exists
Assert glob1 === None                              # VGR: when referenced its value is None
Assert glob1 Is Not Defined                        # VGR: but it is not created by referencing
# VGR: With Add, Subtract, Append, Remove, et al, the undefined value will be zero, [], or {}
#      This is because the variable is an lvalue, not an rvalue as above
Add 1 To glob1
Assert glob1 Is Defined
Assert glob1 === 1
# VGR: The in-place versions of set work similarly
Unset glob1
Set glob1 += "frog"
Assert glob1 == "frog"
# VGR: And the same for key mutations
Unset glob1
Set Key "frog" In glob1 To TRUE
Assert glob1 === {"frog": TRUE}
Unset glob1
Remove Key "frog" From glob1
Assert glob1 Is Undefined                          # VGR: this lacks symmetry with other rvalue usage

# Global variables need a global declaration before they can be used.
# This is a natural consequence of the above, so it would be perfectly reasonable,
# except that globals can’t even be read without an explicit declaration—
# PHP will quietly create a local with the same name, instead.
# I’m not aware of another language with similar scoping issues.
# TODO global/local scope in VGR needs review

# There are no references. What PHP calls references are really aliases;
# there’s nothing that’s a step back, like Perl’s references, and there’s
# no pass-by-object identity like in Python.
# VGR: No aliases/references like PHP or Perl, but we follow Python behavior
#
# Python pass-by-object identity:
# def append_item(items):
#    items.append(4)
# numbers = [1, 2, 3]
# append_item(numbers)
# print(numbers)  # [1, 2, 3, 4]
Define Function append_item(items)
    Append 4 To List items
End-Function
Set numbers To [1, 2, 3]
Call append_item(numbers)
Assert numbers === [1, 2, 3, 4]

# Python "rebinding":
# def replace_list(items):
#    items = [4, 5, 6]
# numbers = [1, 2, 3]
# replace_list(numbers)
# print(numbers)  # [1, 2, 3]
Define Function replace_list(items)
    Set items To [4, 5, 6]
End-Function
Set numbers To [1, 2, 3]
Call replace_list(numbers)
Assert numbers === [1, 2, 3]

# “Referenceness” infects a variable unlike anything else in the language.
# PHP is dynamically-typed, so variables generally have no type… except references,
# which adorn function definitions, variable syntax, and assignment.
# Once a variable is made a reference (which can happen anywhere), it’s stuck as a reference.
# There’s no obvious way to detect this and un-referencing requires nuking the variable entirely.
# VGR: does not apply

# Okay, I lied. There are “SPL types” which also infect variables:
# $x = new SplBool(true); $x = "foo"; will fail. This is like static typing, you see.
# VGR: does not apply

# A reference can be taken to a key that doesn’t exist within an undefined variable (which becomes an array).
# Using a non-existent array normally issues a notice, but this does not.
# VGR: does not apply

# Constants are defined by a function call taking a string;
# before that, they don’t exist. (This may actually be a copy of Perl’s use constant behavior.)
Constant not_variable Is "not_variable"            # VGR: creation of constant by keyword

# Variable names are case-sensitive.
Set a To 3, A To 5
Assert (a == A) == FALSE
Unset a, A

# Function and class names are not. This includes method names, which makes camelCase a
# strange choice for naming.
assert TitleCase("foo") is equal to titlecase("foo")
# VGR: keywords are also case independent, which is very handy (maybe?) for
#      working in the REPL (is it?). Regardless, a large number of scripting language
#      besides PHP, and more "formal" ones too, use case insensitive keywords and/or functions.

# Constructs
# array() and a few dozen similar constructs are not functions.
# array on its own means nothing, $func = "array"; $func(); doesn’t work.

# Array unpacking can be done with the list($a, $b) = ... operation. list() is function-like syntax just like array. I don’t know why this wasn’t given real dedicated syntax, or why the name is so obviously confusing.

# (int) is obviously designed to look like C, but it’s a single token; there’s nothing called int in the language. Try it: not only does var_dump(int) not work, it throws a parse error because the argument looks like the cast operator.
# (integer) is a synonym for (int). There’s also (bool)/(boolean) and (float)/(double)/(real).

# There’s an (array) operator for casting to array and an (object) for casting to object. That sounds nuts, but there’s almost a use: you can use (array) to have a function argument that’s either a single item or a list, and treat it identically. Except you can’t do that reliably, because if someone passes a single object, casting it to an array will actually produce an array containing that object’s attributes. (Casting to object performs the reverse operation.)
# include() and friends are basically C’s #include: they dump another source file into yours. There is no module system, even for PHP code.
# There’s no such thing as a nested or locally-scoped function or class. They’re only global. Including a file dumps its variables into the current function’s scope (and gives the file access to your variables), but dumps functions and classes into global scope.

# Appending to an array is done with $foo[] = $bar.
# echo is a statement-y kind of thing, not a function.
# VGR: Print/Printf are decidely statements. The Terminal and Select statements also produce output.
#      No built-in function by itself produces output.

# empty($var) is so extremely not-a-function that anything but a variable, e.g. empty($var || $var2),
# is a parse error. Why on Earth does the parser need to know about empty? (Fixed in 5.5.)
Assert "".IsEmpty()                                # VGR: IsEmpty() is polymorphic, but primarily works with string, list, dictionary
Assert IsEmpty(" ".Strip())                        # VGR: as a function, it works with expressions

# There’s redundant syntax for blocks: if (...): ... endif;, etc.
# while ($x < 3) {
#    $x++;
# }
# while ($x < 3):
#    $x++;
# endwhile;
# VGR has optional separator following the predicate, variants on the block-ending, including
# omitting that entirely for statement guards
Set x To Zero
While x < 3:  Add 1 To x; End                      # VGR: optional colon/semi, generic end
While x < 4   Add 1 To x  End-While                # VGR: colon/semi omitted, specific end
If x < 5 :    Add 1 To x; End                      # VGR: optional colon/semi, generic end
If x < 6 Then Add 1 To x  End                      # VGR: optional Then, generic end
If x < 7      Add 1 To x  End-If                   # VGR: colon/Then omitted, specific end
If x < 8 ->   Add 1 To x                           # VGR: single guard using "right arrow"
Assert x == 8
